.TH "BASH\-SERVER\-AUTH" "7" "2026\-02\-01" "bash\-server 0.1.0" "bash\-server concepts"
.SH DESCRIPTION
bash\-server uses a shared\-secret token model for client
authentication.
The server generates a cryptographically random token at startup, writes
it to a file with restrictive permissions, and requires every client to
present this token before any other operations are permitted.
.SH CONCEPTS
.SH Token generation
At startup, the server reads 32 bytes of entropy from
\f[CR]/dev/urandom\f[R] and hex\-encodes them to produce a 64\-character
token string.
The raw random bytes are zeroed with \f[CR]memset()\f[R] immediately
after encoding.
.PP
The token is written to a file at \f[CR]<socket_path>.token\f[R] with
permissions \f[CR]0600\f[R] (owner read/write only).
Only processes running as the same user can read the token file.
.SH Token delivery
The token reaches the client through one of these mechanisms:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Token file\f[R]: The client reads \f[CR]<socket_path>.token\f[R]
from the filesystem.
This is the default and most common method.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Auth\-fd mode\f[R] (\f[CR]\-\-auth\-fd N\f[R]): The server writes
the token to file descriptor N instead of stderr.
This is designed for parent processes that spawn the server and want to
receive the token programmatically without filesystem round\-trips.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Stderr\f[R] (default when no \f[CR]\-\-auth\-fd\f[R]): The server
prints the token to stderr for manual use or capture by a parent
process.
.SH v1 authentication
In the v1 text protocol, authentication is a single request\-response
exchange:
.IP
.EX
Client: AUTH <hex\-token>\(rsn
Server: OK\(rsn              (on success)
Server: ERR auth_failed\(rsn (on failure)
.EE
.PP
The token is sent as a plain hex string.
The server compares it using constant\-time comparison.
.SH v2 authentication
In the v2 protocol (binary or NDJSON), authentication uses JSON messages
on CHAN_CONTROL:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]Client:\f[R] {\(dqtype\(dq:\(dqauth\(dq,\(dqtoken\(dq:\(dq<hex\-token>\(dq}
\f[B]Server:\f[R] {\(dqtype\(dq:\(dqauth_ok\(dq}
\f[B]Server:\f[R] {\(dqtype\(dq:\(dqauth_fail\(dq,\(dqreason\(dq:\(dqinvalid token\(dq}
.EE
.SH Constant\-time comparison
Token comparison uses \f[CR]protocol_secure_compare()\f[R], which
examines every byte of both strings regardless of where a mismatch
occurs.
This prevents timing side\-channel attacks where an attacker could
deduce token characters by measuring response times.
.PP
The implementation XORs corresponding bytes and accumulates the result,
then checks whether the accumulated value is zero.
Both strings must also be the same length.
.SH Cygwin SO_PEERCRED handshake
On Cygwin, Unix domain sockets are implemented over TCP loopback.
During \f[CR]connect()\f[R]/\f[CR]accept()\f[R], Cygwin performs a
credential handshake to exchange \f[CR]ucred\f[R] structures.
.PP
This handshake can conflict with non\-blocking connect patterns used by
Python and other high\-level socket libraries, causing
\f[CR]ECONNABORTED\f[R] (errno 113) on \f[CR]accept()\f[R].
.PP
The \f[CR]\-\-no\-peercred\f[R] flag calls:
.IP
.EX
setsockopt(fd, SOL_SOCKET, SO_PEERCRED, NULL, 0);
.EE
.PP
This disables the credential handshake entirely.
The \f[CR]NULL, 0\f[R] argument form is required; any non\-NULL optval
or non\-zero optlen returns \f[CR]EINVAL\f[R].
.PP
\f[B]Trade\-off\f[R]: \f[CR]getpeereid()\f[R] and
\f[CR]getsockopt(SO_PEERCRED)\f[R] no longer return peer credentials.
This is acceptable because bash\-server relies on token\-based
authentication, not peer credential checks.
.SH Pre\-authentication restrictions
Before successful authentication, the server only accepts:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]AUTH\f[R] (v1) or \f[CR]auth\f[R] on CHAN_CONTROL (v2)
.PP
All other commands or channel messages are rejected until the client has
authenticated.
.SH Session initialization
On the first successful authentication in a server\(cqs lifetime, the
server calls \f[CR]init_bash_for_session()\f[R] to initialize the Bash
interpreter: builtins, traps, signals, tilde expansion, shell variables,
job control stubs, input subsystem, and shell options.
This initialization is performed once and is guarded by a
\f[CR]bash_initialized\f[R] flag.
